Closes the two residual gaps INV-hazov's own audit found. The mechanism
landed earlier; these are the parts it could not see.

(a) THE ALIAS SHAPE. Rule 1 matched only `<expr>.meta["k"] = ...`, so a
bare name defeated it outright:

    meta = symbol.meta          # an ALIAS -- the same dict object
    meta["concepts"] = kept     # invisible to rule 1

Exactly one live site used it, in framework_patterns -- the same module
that produced this invariant's seventh instance. A COPY IS NOT AN ALIAS:
`m = dict(x.meta)` builds a fresh dict and destroys nothing until it is
assigned back, which is already rule 3's business. Measured over the
tree: 1 alias, 13 copies, and the 13 are the control showing the
predicate is not simply matching every local dict.

(b) THE REMOVAL CASE, which is why that site had to bypass at all. It
REMOVES concepts a later-computed fact invalidates, and `concepts` is
declared merge_union -- so write_meta_key would have unioned the
stripped entries straight back in. The vocabulary had add/refine/replace
and no remove, so the one legitimate remover in the tree could not route
through the chokepoint, and the linter could not tell that bypass from a
careless assignment.

The answer is a second VERB, not a new discipline. A write_discipline
says how several WRITERS COMBINE, and `concepts` genuinely is
merge_union for its writers -- two enrichment passes must keep both. A
curator is not a competing writer: it runs after them and narrows their
agreed result. filter_meta_key() is that entry point, restricted to
merge_union keys because a single_writer / producer_primary key holds
one authoritative value and dropping it is the erasure this class is
about. The two rejected alternatives (a `removal` discipline value,
false about the writers; an audited-exception list, which leaves the
bypass invisible) are recorded in the docstring rather than left to be
re-litigated.

A LATENT BUG IN RULE 3 FELL OUT OF WRITING THE ALIAS TEST. `_scopes`
returns the module plus every function, but ast.walk FROM THE MODULE
descends into every function body -- so "per scope" was not per scope,
and a name bound in one function could match a subscript in another.
That is the identical module-wide false positive `_scopes` was
introduced to kill, surviving only because no module happened to pair
the two shapes. One bounded walker now serves both rules.

Controls: the checker is CLEAN on the live tree and FIRES on the site
with the fix reverted; the cross-scope and dict-copy cases are pinned by
their own tests; collision-capable keys stay 5, all declared.

Ratchet: this change breached +1 (attr-defined, `ast.AST` has no
lineno). Mine, fixed at source by narrowing to ast.Assign rather than
raising the baseline. 672, delta 0.

